{"absent":["Keyring operation argument order, return values, error codes and permission checks beyond numeric operation identities.","Keyring capability query output layout and watch notification payload format.","Landlock syscall numbers, enforcement semantics, layering and audit behavior beyond header constants and attribute layouts.","Conditions selecting each libc-compat alternative definition branch.","Live update session lifecycle, token allocation, descriptor ownership and failure handling beyond ioctl numbers and payload layouts.","LoadPin digest list encoding and authorization requirements.","LSM syscall interface, context serialization for multiple modules, and flag combinations beyond single-entry flag."],"dossier":"D17","findings":[{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_THREAD_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_PROCESS_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_SESSION_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_USER_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_USER_SESSION_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_GROUP_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_REQKEY_AUTH_KEY","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_SPEC_REQUESTOR_KEYRING"],"text":"Special keyring selectors are negative serials descending from minus one to minus eight in thread, process, session, user, user-session, group, request auth, then requestor order."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_NO_CHANGE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_DEFAULT","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_THREAD_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_PROCESS_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_SESSION_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_USER_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_USER_SESSION_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_GROUP_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEY_REQKEY_DEFL_REQUESTOR_KEYRING"],"text":"Default request-key destination selectors use minus one for no change, zero for default, then one through seven for thread, process, session, user, user-session, group and requestor."},{"kind":"entry_point","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_GET_KEYRING_ID","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_JOIN_SESSION_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_UPDATE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_REVOKE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CLEAR","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_LINK","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_SEARCH","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_READ","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_INSTANTIATE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_SET_TIMEOUT","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_INVALIDATE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_GET_PERSISTENT","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_DH_COMPUTE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_PKEY_QUERY","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_RESTRICT_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_MOVE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPABILITIES","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_WATCH_KEY"],"text":"Key control multiplexor operations are numbered zero through thirty two, covering identity lookup, session join, update, revoke, ownership, permission, describe, clear, link, unlink, search, read, instantiate, negate, default selection, timeout, authority assumption, security label, parent session, reject, vector instantiate, invalidate, persistent, Diffie-Hellman, four public key ops, restriction, move, capabilities and watch."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_dh_params","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_dh_params.anon_1"],"text":"Diffie-Hellman parameters carry three signed 32 bit key serials for private value, prime and base, where the private slot offers two alternative member names sharing one union."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_kdf_params"],"text":"Key derivation parameters carry two user pointers for hash name and other info, a 32 bit other-info length, and eight spare 32 bit words."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_SUPPORTS_ENCRYPT","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_SUPPORTS_DECRYPT","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_SUPPORTS_SIGN","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_SUPPORTS_VERIFY"],"text":"Public key capability bits are one for encrypt, two for decrypt, four for sign and eight for verify."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_pkey_query"],"text":"Public key query result carries supported operation mask and key size as 32 bit values, four 16 bit maxima for data, signature, encrypted and decrypted sizes, plus ten spare 32 bit words."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_pkey_params","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_pkey_params.anon_1"],"text":"Public key operation parameters carry a signed key serial, a 32 bit input length, a union holding either output length or second input length, and seven spare 32 bit words."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_MOVE_EXCL"],"text":"Move exclusive flag is value one and requests no displacement from the destination ring."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_CAPABILITIES","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_PERSISTENT_KEYRINGS","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_DIFFIE_HELLMAN","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_PUBLIC_KEY","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_BIG_KEY","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_INVALIDATE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_RESTRICT_KEYRING","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS0_MOVE","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS1_NS_KEYRING_NAME","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS1_NS_KEY_TAG","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::KEYCTL_CAPS1_NOTIFICATIONS"],"text":"First capabilities word uses bits one through 128 for capabilities query, persistent rings, Diffie-Hellman, public key, big key, invalidate, restrict and move; second word uses bits one, two and four for namespaced ring names, namespaced key tags and watchable notifications."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::landlock_ruleset_attr"],"text":"Landlock ruleset attributes are six unsigned 64 bit masks in handled filesystem, handled network, scoped, quiet filesystem, quiet network and quiet scoped order."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_CREATE_RULESET_VERSION","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_CREATE_RULESET_ERRATA","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ADD_RULE_QUIET","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_RESTRICT_SELF_LOG_SAME_EXEC_OFF","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_RESTRICT_SELF_LOG_NEW_EXEC_ON","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_RESTRICT_SELF_LOG_SUBDOMAINS_OFF","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_RESTRICT_SELF_TSYNC","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_RESTRICT_SELF_NO_NEW_PRIVS"],"text":"Ruleset creation flags are bit zero for version query and bit one for errata query; add-rule quiet is bit zero; self-restriction flags occupy bits zero through four for same-exec log off, new-exec log on, subdomain log off, thread sync and no-new-privs."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::landlock_rule_type"],"text":"Landlock rule type enumeration assigns one to path-beneath rules and the next value to network port rules."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::landlock_path_beneath_attr"],"text":"Path-beneath rule carries an unsigned 64 bit allowed access mask and a signed 32 bit parent descriptor, packed with no trailing padding."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::landlock_net_port_attr"],"text":"Network port rule carries an unsigned 64 bit allowed access mask and an unsigned 64 bit port in host byte order, where zero authorizes binding to an automatically assigned ephemeral port."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_EXECUTE","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_WRITE_FILE","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_READ_FILE","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_READ_DIR","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REMOVE_DIR","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REMOVE_FILE","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_CHAR","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_DIR","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_REG","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_SOCK","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_FIFO","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_BLOCK","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_SYM","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REFER","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_TRUNCATE","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_IOCTL_DEV","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_RESOLVE_UNIX"],"text":"Filesystem access rights are single-bit masks from bit zero through bit sixteen for execute, write file, read file, read directory, remove directory, remove file, make character, directory, regular, socket, fifo, block, symlink, refer, truncate, device ioctl and Unix socket resolution."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_NET_BIND_TCP","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_NET_CONNECT_TCP","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_NET_BIND_UDP","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_ACCESS_NET_CONNECT_SEND_UDP","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_SCOPE_ABSTRACT_UNIX_SOCKET","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::LANDLOCK_SCOPE_SIGNAL"],"text":"Network access rights use low bits zero through three for TCP bind, TCP connect, UDP bind and UDP connect-send; scoping rights use bit zero for abstract Unix sockets and bit one for signals."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_IF_IFCONF.alt_1","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_IF_IFCONF.alt_2","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_IF_IFCONF.alt_3","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_IN_ADDR.alt_1","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_IN_ADDR.alt_2","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_IN_ADDR.alt_3","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_SOCKADDR_IN6.alt_1","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_SOCKADDR_IN6.alt_3","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_XATTR.alt_1","include/uapi/linux/libc-compat.h::__UAPI_DEF_XATTR.alt_3"],"text":"Libc compatibility macros select whether kernel headers define network interface, address, protocol, packet info, request, class, IPv6, and extended attribute structures, with zero suppressing the kernel definition when libc provides it and one enabling it."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/limits.h::_UAPI_LINUX_LIMITS_H","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::NR_OPEN","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::NGROUPS_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::ARG_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::LINK_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::MAX_CANON","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::MAX_INPUT","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::NAME_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::PATH_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::PIPE_BUF","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::XATTR_NAME_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::XATTR_SIZE_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::XATTR_LIST_MAX","include/uapi/linux/limits.h::RTSIG_MAX"],"text":"System limits define open files 1024, supplemental groups 65536, argument plus environment bytes 131072, link count 127, canonical and input queue sizes 255, name length 255, path length including terminator 4096, atomic pipe write 4096, extended attribute name 255 with value and list maxima 65536, and queued realtime signals 32."},{"kind":"entry_point","sids":["include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::LIVEUPDATE_IOCTL_TYPE","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::anon_1","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::anon_2","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_ioctl_create_session","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_ioctl_retrieve_session","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_preserve_fd","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_retrieve_fd","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_finish","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_get_name"],"text":"Live update uses ioctl type hex BA with base commands zero and one for session creation and retrieval, and session commands hex 40 through 43 for preserving, retrieving, finishing and naming a session."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::LIVEUPDATE_SESSION_NAME_LENGTH","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_ioctl_create_session","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_ioctl_retrieve_session","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_preserve_fd","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_retrieve_fd","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_finish","include/uapi/linux/liveupdate.h::liveupdate_session_get_name"],"text":"Live update payloads all start with a 32 bit size; create and retrieve carry a descriptor plus a 64 byte session name, preserve and retrieve descriptors carry a descriptor plus an aligned 64 bit token, finish carries a reserved word, and get-name carries a reserved word plus a 64 byte name."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/loadpin.h::_UAPI_LINUX_LOOP_LOADPIN_H","include/uapi/linux/loadpin.h::LOADPIN_IOC_SET_TRUSTED_VERITY_DIGESTS"],"text":"LoadPin control uses magic byte L with command zero writing an unsigned integer carrying trusted verity digests."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::lsm_ctx"],"text":"Security module context carries four unsigned 64 bit header fields for module identity, flags, total length and payload length, followed by a flexible payload whose bound is the payload length field."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_UNDEF","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_CAPABILITY","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_SELINUX","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_SMACK","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_TOMOYO","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_APPARMOR","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_YAMA","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_LOADPIN","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_SAFESETID","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_LOCKDOWN","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_BPF","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_LANDLOCK","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_IMA","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_EVM","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ID_IPE"],"text":"Security module identities use zero for undefined and 100 through 113 for capability, SELinux, Smack, Tomoyo, AppArmor, Yama, LoadPin, Safesetid, Lockdown, BPF, Landlock, IMA, EVM and IPE."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_UNDEF","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_CURRENT","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_EXEC","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_FSCREATE","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_KEYCREATE","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_PREV","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_ATTR_SOCKCREATE","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::LSM_FLAG_SINGLE"],"text":"Security attribute selectors use zero for undefined and 100 through 105 for current, exec, filesystem create, key create, previous and socket create; single-entry retrieval flag is hex one."},{"kind":"hazard","sids":["include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_kdf_params","include/uapi/linux/keyctl.h::keyctl_pkey_query","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::landlock_path_beneath_attr","include/uapi/linux/landlock.h::landlock_net_port_attr","include/uapi/linux/lsm.h::lsm_ctx"],"text":"Callers must validate user memory pointers for derivation strings, respect flexible payload length against total length, account for packed path rule layout without implicit padding, treat ports as host order, and size buffers from query maxima rather than assuming fixed sizes."}],"index_id":"99dbddb713